===Etymology===
{{bor+|tl|ar|أَبَق|t=abaca}}, from {{der|tl|syc|ܐܳܦܰܩܬܳܐ|tr=ʾāpaqtā}}, {{m|syc|ܐܰܦܩܰܥܬܳܐ|tr=ʾap̄qaʿtā|t=de-seeded cotton}}. According to Potet (2013), Muslim marine merchants imposed their term on their suppliers so that native terms died out in the Philippines.

===Pronunciation===
{{tl-pr|abaká}}

===Noun===
{{tl-noun|abaká|b=+}}

# {{C|tl|Fibers|Zingiberales order plants}} [[Manila hemp]]; [[abaca]]
#: {{ux|tl|Si Pedro ay gumamit ng '''abaka''' sa pagtali ng kahoy.|Pedro used '''abaca''' to tie the wood together.}}

====Descendants====
* {{desc|gl|abacá|bor=1}}
* {{desc|ms|abaka|bor=1}}
* {{desc|pt|abacá|bor=1}}
* {{desctree|bor=1|es|abacá}}

===References===
* {{R:tl:Potet|APL|page=131}}
