Page 1199 apat: Skipped, no changes
Page 1199 apat: -------- begin text --------
{{number box|tl}}

===Etymology===
From Proto-Central Philippine ''*a-əpat'', from {{inh|tl|phi-pro|*əpat|*a-əpat}}, from {{inh|tl|poz-pro|*əpat|*a-əpat}}, from {{inh|tl|map-pro|*Səpat|*Sa-Səpat}}. {{doublet|tl|ipat}}.

===Pronunciation===
{{tl-pr|+<audio:Tl-apat.oga>}}

===Numeral===
{{tl-num|b=+}}

# {{cln|tl|cardinal numbers}} [[four]]
#: {{syn|tl|kuwatro|ipat|q2=obsolete|agyo|q3=obsolete}}

====Usage notes====
* To describe the quantity of something, the number is placed before the noun and affixed with a '''-ng''' when the word ends with a vowel, and a separate word '''[[na]]''' for a consonant.
*: {{ux|tl|'''Isang''' saging, '''dalawang''' pinya|'''One''' banana, '''two''' pineapples}}
*: {{ux|tl|'''Apat na''' mansanas, '''anim na''' mangga''|'''Four''' apples, '''six''' mangos}}

====Derived terms====
{{der3|tl|apatan|apat-apat|apatin|apatnaraan|pag-apatin|apatnapu|ikaapat|pang-apat|ikaapatnapu|labing-apat|makaapat|mag-apat|sangkapat|tig-apat|saikapat|ikaapat na kapangyarihan|sikapat}}

===Further reading===
* {{R:Pambansang Diksiyonaryo}}
* {{R:map:ACD|Sa-Sepat|a-epat}}

{{C|tl|Four}}

