Page 1845 bahaghari: -------- begin text --------
[[Image:WhereRainbowRises.jpg|thumb|isang '''bahaghari''']]

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|tl|bahag-hari}}
* {{alt|tl|balaghari||Eastern Marinduque}}

===Etymology===
From {{compound|tl|bahag|t1=loincloth|hari|t2=king}}. However, according to Wolff (1976), the latter component is from {{der|tl|ms|hari||day}},<ref>{{cite-book|year=1976|last=Wolff|first=John U.|chapter=Malay borrowings in Tagalog|title=Southeast Asian History and Historiography: Essays Presented to D. G. E. Hall|editors=C.D. Cowan & O.W. Wolters|location=Ithaca|publisher=Cornell University Press|url=https://zorc.net/RDZorc/TAGALOG/Wolff-1976-MalayBorrowingsInTagalog.pdf|page=356}}</ref> whereas for Potet (2016), the latter comes from {{der|tl|sa|हरि||the sun}}.<ref>{{R:tl:Potet|TBC|page=283}}</ref> Compare {{cog|agv|balaghadi}} and {{cog|pam|pinanari}}.

===Pronunciation===
{{tl-pr|bahagharì}}

===Noun===
{{tl-noun|bahagharì|b=+}}

# [[rainbow]]
#: {{syn|tl|balangaw|balantok|bahagsubay|arkuiris}}

===References===
<references/>

{{C|tl|Light|Atmospheric phenomena}}
{{cln|tl|compound nouns}}
