Page 2393 barya: Removing explicit syllabification same as auto-syllabification: {{hyph|tl|bar|ya}}
Page 2393 barya: Replaced {{tl-IPA|baryá}} with {{tl-pr|baryá}}
Page 2393 barya: Would save with comment = convert {{tl-IPA}} to {{tl-pr}}; remove {{hyph}} same as respelling auto-syllabification
Page 2393 barya: -------- begin text --------

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|tl|bariya}}

===Etymology===
From an earlier variant, {{m|tl|bariya}}, from {{w|Early Modern Spanish|Early Modern}} {{bor|tl|es|barilla}} which used to refer to the Spanish-era copper coins minted in the Philippines. The Spanish {{angbr|ll}} became Tagalog {{angbr|y}}, due to {{w|yeísmo}} of Philippine Spanish speakers during the early to middle Spanish colonial period, and the stress shifted to the last syllable, from {{IPAchar|/baˈɾija/}} to {{IPAchar|/baɾiˈja/}}, and the {{angbr|i}} between {{angbr|r}} and {{angbr|y}} was elided.

Some sources, like Zorc (1979), also suggest it to be from {{ncog|es|varia}}, through {{m|es|variar|t=to change; to alter}}, which Potet (2016) notes as a mistake during the {{w|History of the Philippines (1898–1946)|American colonial era}} by interpreting the word to mean "to change into small coins; to exchange". However, Potet (2016) notes that the Spanish term for loose change is {{ncog|es|vuelta}}.

===Pronunciation===
{{tl-pr|baryá}}

===Noun===
{{tl-noun|baryá|b=+}}

# [[loose change]] {{gl|small, loose money in coins or notes of small denominations}}
#: {{syn|tl|sensilyo|mulay|muyag}}
#: {{ux|tl|'''Barya''' lang po ang tinatanggap sa umaga.|Only '''loose change''' will be accepted in the morning.}}

====Derived terms====
{{der3|tl|baryahin|magbarya|pambarya|ibarya|pagbabarya}}

====See also====
{{col3|tl|sukli}}

===Further reading===
* {{R:KWF Diksiyonaryo}}
* {{R:tl:Potet|NUOT|page=139}}
* {{R:CEDOF|1|42}}
-------- end text --------
