Page 3503 buryong: Removing explicit syllabification same as auto-syllabification: {{hyph|tl|bur|yong}}
Page 3503 buryong: Replaced {{tl-IPA|buryóng}} with {{tl-pr|buryóng}}
Page 3503 buryong: Would save with comment = convert {{tl-IPA}} to {{tl-pr}}; remove {{hyph}} same as respelling auto-syllabification
Page 3503 buryong: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
{{bor+|tl|war}}. Originated from 1980s prison slang, referring to the fits of insanity by prisoners who have been incarcerated for long without contact with family and the outside world. {{rfe|tl|date=December 2020}}.

===Pronunciation===
{{tl-pr|buryóng}}

===Adjective===
{{tl-adj|buryóng|b=+}}

# {{lb|tl|originally|prison slang}} [[bored]] and [[irritated]] from being [[confined]] or [[locked up]]; suffering from [[cabin fever]]

===Noun===
{{tl-noun|buryóng|b=+}}

# {{lb|tl|slang|specifically}} [[boredom]] and [[irritability]] resulting from [[incarceration]]
#* {{quote-text|tl|year=2002|title=Ani
|passage=Maayos naman ang lahat: hindi malamig ang gabi at hindi bilog ang buwan;  walang '''buryong''' o kalungkutang tinatakasan; walang insomyang inililibang;  walang kataksilang pinaghihigantian.
|translation=Everything's fine: the night is not cool and the moon is not round; there is no '''boredom''' or loneliness to escape from; there is no insomnia to waste one's time; there is no treachery to avenge.}}
#* {{quote-text|tl|year=2006|author=Jun Cruz Reyes|title=Armando
|passage=Hindi tama ang malaking problema para sa isang kadre, kundi ang '''buryong'''.
|translation=Insanity is not a big problem for a cadre, but '''boredom''' and irritability from being confined.}}
# {{lb|tl|by extension}} [[cabin fever]]

====Derived terms====
{{der3|tl|ikaburyong|maburyong}}
-------- end text --------
