Page 4750 dito: Removing explicit syllabification same as auto-syllabification: {{hyph|tl|di|to}}
Page 4750 dito: Replaced {{tl-IPA}} with {{tl-pr}}
Page 4750 dito: Would save with comment = convert {{tl-IPA}} to {{tl-pr}}; remove {{hyph}} same as respelling auto-syllabification
Page 4750 dito: -------- begin text --------

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|tl|rito}}
* {{alt|tl|d2||text messaging}}

===Etymology===
From Western {{inh|tl|poz-pro|*di-tu}},<ref>{{R:map:ACD|-Cu|di-tu}}</ref> from {{m|poz-pro|*di}} + {{m|poz-pro|*-tu}}, from {{der|tl|map-pro|*Cu}}. The latter half of the word is likely related to {{m|tl|ito}}, in a similar pattern to other Tagalog demonstrative pronouns. Compare {{cog|ceb|didto}} and {{cog|ilo|ditoy}}. Meanwhile, the former half is possibly related to {{cog|ms|di}} and {{cog|id|di}} as a likely cognate.

===Pronunciation===
{{tl-pr}}

===Adverb===
{{tl-adv|b=+}}

# [[here]] {{gl|near the speaker and the listener}}
# [[here]] {{gl|near the speaker}}
#: {{syn|tl|dine|q1=dialectal|rine|q2=dialectal}}

====Usage notes====
* When the preceding word ends with a vowel, {{angbr|w}}, or {{angbr|y}}, {{m|tl|rito}} is used instead, but the distinction isn't always made. Other words with this phenomenon include {{m|tl|diyan}}, {{m|tl|doon}}, {{m|tl|daw}}, and {{m|tl|din}}.

====Derived terms====
{{der3|tl|bukod sa rito|dumito|narito|naririto|nandito|pumarito|pagparito|paparituhin|parituhan|parito|tagarito}}

====See also====
{{tl-dem pronouns}}

===References===
<references/>
