Page 6057 g̃: Already saw {{tl-pr}}, skipping: {{tl-pr}}
Page 6057 g̃: Skipped, no changes
Page 6057 g̃: -------- begin text --------

===Pronunciation===
{{tl-pr}}

===Letter===
{{tl-letter|b=nga}}

# {{tlb|tl|archaic}} {{n-g|the letter {{m|tl|g}} with a [[tilde]]}}

====Usage notes====
* The tilde indicated that the g is supposed to be a nasal sound. The letter represented the velar nasal {{IPAchar|/ŋ/}} when the Spanish-based orthography was used. In the newer orthographies, the digraph {{m|tl|ng}} (treated as a letter in the newer alphabets) represented the sound.
* The letter usually written an {{angbr|n}} before it,  For example: ''ba'''ng̃'''o'' ({{m|tl|bango}}, “fragrance”). However, the letter can also stand by itself as in ''ba'''g̃'''o''.
* Without the tilde, the {{angbr|ng}} will be understood as {{IPAchar|/ŋɡ/}} in the Spanish-based orthography and can be interpreted differently. For example, the difference between ''man'''g̃'''a'' ({{m|tl|manga}}/{{m|tl|mga}}, “plural marker”) and ''man'''g'''a'' ({{m|tl|mangga}}, “mango”). Although, in rare instances, {{angbr|g̃g}} can be found written together for {{IPAchar|/ŋɡ/}} as in: ''ma'''g̃g'''a''.
* If the {{angbr|ng̃}} does not follow a vowel, it can already be understood as the nasal sound. For example, ''binasa'''ng̃''' aclat'' can simply be written as ''binasa'''ng''' aclat'' ({{m|tl|[[basa|binasa]]ng [[aklat]]}}, “read book”). However, one should still write it as ''binasa'''g̃''' aclat'' so that it does not get interpreted as ''binasa'''g''' aclat'' (“fractured, book”).
* Since a tilde above vowels (or {{angbr|m}} and {{angbr|n}}, ex. {{m|osp|anno}} → {{m|es|año}}) in old Spanish orthographies represented an implicit letter with a nasal sound ({{angbr|m}}, {{angbr|n}}, {{angbr|g̃}}) after the said vowel, para'''ng''' can also be written as par'''ãg''' or par'''ã'''.
* Besides {{angbr|g}}, the velar nasal {{IPAchar|/ŋ/}} is already implied when {{angbr|n}} is before the letter {{angbr|c}}, and {{angbr|g̃}} does not need to be written in the Spanish-based orthography. For example: ''banca'' ({{m|tl|bangka}}, “boat”).
* Sometimes, {{angbr|ng̃}} is being written as {{angbr|ñg}} or {{angbr|g̃n}} but still understood as the velar nasal sound.
* The letter is called {{m|es|gangosa||nasal; nosy}} in old Tagalog texts.

====See also====
{{col3|tl|g|ng}}
-------- end text --------
