===Etymology===
{{bor+|tl|nan-hbl|福建|tr=Hok-kiàn|t=[[Fujian]] / [[Fukien]]}}. {{doublet|tl|Fukien}}.

===Pronunciation===
{{tl-pr}}

===Proper noun===
{{tl-proper noun|b=+}}

# the {{l|en|Hokkien}} language {{gl|linguistic variety under [[Southern Min]] of the [[Min]] languages of the [[Sinitic]] ([[Chinese]]) languages of the {{w|Sino-Tibetan}} family}}
#: {{ux|tl|wikang '''Hokkien'''|Language of Hokkien}}
#: {{ux|tl|salitang '''Hokkien'''|Hokkien language word; Hokkien Language}}
#* {{quote-book|tl|year=1996|author=Alfredo E. Litiatco|author2=Isagani R. Cruz|title=The Alfredo E. Litiatco lectures of Isagani R. Cruz|publisher=De La Salle University Press|isbn=9789715551137|page=237|url=https://books.google.com.ph/books?id=JrVkAAAAMAAJ&q=salitang+%22Hokkien%22&dq=salitang+%22Hokkien%22&hl=en&newbks=1&newbks_redir=0&sa=X&ved=2ahUKEwjH9se60J6FAxXy8zgGHWcAC9IQ6AF6BAgHEAI
|text={{nb...}} Iyan ang historikal na dahilan kung bakit hindi pinapansin ng halos lahat ng mga kritiko ang literaturang Tsino natin. Kahit na humigit kumulang sa dalawang daang Filipino ang nagsusulat sa wikang Mandarin o '''Hokkien''' sa kasalukuyan ay hindi inaakala ng mga bobong iskolar na dugong Malay na malaking bahagi ng literatura{{nb...}}|t={{nb...}} That is the historical reason why almost all critics ignore our Chinese literature. Even though there are about two hundred Filipinos writing in Mandarin or '''Hokkien''' today, stupid scholars of Malay blood do not think that a large part of the literature {{nb...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|tl|year=1997|author=Joaquin Sy|title=Tsapsuy: mga sanaysay, tula, salin at iba pa 雜碎|publisher=Kaisa Para sa Kaunlaran|isbn=9789718857144|pages=260,265|url=https://books.google.com.ph/books?newbks=1&newbks_redir=0&id=XZBkAAAAMAAJ&dq=sa+%22Hokkien%22&focus=searchwithinvolume&q=%22Hokkien%22
|text={{nb...}} at lalim ng impluwensiyang Tsino sa wikang Tagalog. Dito'y dapat linawin na ang mga bokabularyong Tsinong tinukoy sa pag-aaral ay mula sa wikang '''Hokkien'''. Mangilan-ngilan lamang ang mula sa Cantones at Mandarin. Hindi naman ito kataka-taka dahil mahigit nobenta porsiyento ng mga Tsinong lumikas sa Pilipinas ay galing sa Fujian (o Hokkian) {{nb...}} At ang salitang "paslang," ayon sa pag-aaral ni Manuel, ay mula sa pariralang '''Hokkien''' na ''"phah si lang"'' na ang kahuluga'y... ano pa nga ba kundi "pumatay ng tao!" {{nb...}}|t={{nb...}} and depth of Chinese influence on the Tagalog language. Here it should be clarified that the Chinese vocabulary referred to in the study is from the '''Hokkien''' language. Only a few come from Cantonese and Mandarin. This is not surprising because more than ninety percent of the Chinese who emigrated to the Philippines are from Fujian (or Hokkian) {{nb...}} And the word "paslang," according to Manuel's study, is from the '''Hokkien''' phrase ''"phah si lang"'' which means... what else but "to kill a person!" {{nb...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|tl|year=2003|trans-title=Flowers of May: Three Selected Stories|title=五月花節：柯清淡小說精選 (Flores de Mayo: Tatlong Piling Kwento)|author=Ching Tam Cua|translator=Joaquin Sy|publisher=Kaisa Para sa Kaunlaran|isbn=9789718857304|page=48,50|url=https://books.google.com.ph/books?id=hJtkAAAAMAAJ&q=salitang+%22Hokkien%22&dq=salitang+%22Hokkien%22&hl=en&newbks=1&newbks_redir=0&sa=X&ved=2ahUKEwjH9se60J6FAxXy8zgGHWcAC9IQ6AF6BAgFEAI
|text={{nb...}} lumabas mula sa kuwarto ang anak kong lalaking nakabihis militar at may hawak na riple. Pinisil niya ako sa balikat saka sinabi sa wikang '''Hokkien''', "Magsasanay kaming magpaputok ng rifle ngayong araw. Pa! Corporal na ako, hindi na flag bearer {{nb...}} '''Hokkien''' ang dalawang salitang iyon. Pagkatapos ng anak kong lalaki sa paaralang Tsino, nagkolehiyo na siya sa paaralang Pilipino. Nag-aalala akong makakalimutan niya ang wikang Tsino, pati na ang sulat Tsino. Nang maglaho sa labas {{nb...}} usap-usapan ng mga tao sa Chinatown, ang pangongolekta ng donasyon ng class president naming binansagan naming Kuya Macao na bagama't Cantones ay nakapagsasalita ng matatas na '''Hokkien''', ang limang linggong wala akong perang manood ng {{nb...}}|t={{nb...}} my son came out of the room in military uniform and holding a rifle. He squeezed my shoulder and said in '''Hokkien''', "We're going to practice firing a rifle today. Pa! I'm a corporal, no longer a flag bearer {{nb...}} Those two words are '''Hokkien'''. After my son went to Chinese school, he went to college in the Filipino school. I was worried that he would forget the Chinese language, including the Chinese script. When he disappeared outside {{nb...}} the people in Chinatown talked about the donation collection of our class president nicknamed Brother Macau that although Cantonese can speak fluent '''Hokkien''', the five weeks I didn't have money to watch {{nb...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|tl|year=2004|trans-title=Babél: Poems of Mayo Uno Martin|title=Babél: Mga Tula ni Mayo Uno Martin|author=Mayo Uno Martin|publisher=High Chair|isbn=9789719278016|page=41|url=https://books.google.com.ph/books?id=_I1kAAAAMAAJ&q=salitang+%22Hokkien%22&dq=salitang+%22Hokkien%22&hl=en&newbks=1&newbks_redir=0&sa=X&ved=2ahUKEwjH9se60J6FAxXy8zgGHWcAC9IQ6AF6BAgEEAI
|text={{nb...}} Napanaginipan ng historyador ang emperador. Nagpakilala itong si Shih Huang Ti. (Nakakapag-'''Hokkien''' sa panaginip.) Matapos libutin ang maharlikang hardin, {{nb...}}|t={{nb...}} The historian dreamed of the emperor. He introduced himself as Shih Huang Ti. (Able to use '''Hokkien''' in a dream.) After touring the royal garden, {{nb...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|tl|year=2005|chapter=Ugat ng Huweteng|title=Sawikaan 2005: Mga Salita ng Taon|publisher=UP Press|isbn=9789715425155|page=4|url=https://books.google.com.ph/books?id=-N-cNZ7fUOYC&pg=PA4&dq=%22wikang+Amoy%22&hl=en&newbks=1&newbks_redir=0&sa=X&ved=2ahUKEwjFivrxzZ6FAxWASGcHHUyhA6MQ6AF6BAgIEAI#v=onepage&q=%22wikang%20Amoy%22&f=false
|text={{nb...}}Hango umano ang "huweteng" ["hue"+"eng" o "teng"] sa Tsino, at iniuugnay sa wikang Amoy-'''Hokkien'''. Ayon sa PCIJ, ang "hue" daw ay may literal na katumbas na "bulaklak" [flower], samantalang ang "eng" o "teng" ay katumbas daw ng "pusta" o "taya" [bet].{{nb...}} Ayon kay Joaquin Sy, isang eksperto sa '''Hokkien''' at tagasalin ng mga akdang Tsino, ang "huweteng" ay maaaring mula sa "hua" [bulaklak] at "tang" [salansan ng papeles]. Wala namang makapagsasabi kung sa paglipas ng panahon ay nagbago ang bigkas sa "hua tang" at naging "hue teng." Kung babalikan naman ang ''Tsapsuy: Mga sanaysay, tula, salin, at iba pa'' (1997) ni Sy, binanggit ang "jue xin" na mulang '''Hokkien'''. Tumutukoy ang "jue xin" sa tao na buo {{nb...}}|t={{nb...}}It is indeed similar as said that "huweteng" ["hue"+"eng" or "teng"] is derived from Chinese, and is associated with the Amoy-'''Hokkien''' language. According to the PCIJ, "hue" is said to have a literal equivalent to "bulaklak" [flower], while "eng" or "teng" is said to be equivalent to "pusta" or "taya" [bet].{{nb...}} According to Joaquin Sy, a '''Hokkien''' expert and translator of Chinese works, "hueteng" may be from "hua" [flower] and "tang" [stack of papers]. No one can say if over time the pronunciation changed from "hua tang" to "hue teng." Going back to Sy's ''Tsapsuy: Essays, poems, translations, and others'' (1997), "jue xin" from '''Hokkien''' is mentioned. "jue xin" refers to the person as a whole}}
#* {{quote-book|tl|year=2007|trans-title=Wandering the South Seas (Southeast Asia)|title=Lagalag sa Nanyang: (Nanyang Piaoliuji) 南洋漂流記|author=Bai Ren 白刃|translator=Joaquin Sy 施華謹|publisher=UP Press|isbn=9789715425353|page=334|url=https://books.google.com.ph/books?id=VsG7EMyYfzUC&pg=PA334&dq=salitang+%22Hokkien%22&hl=en&newbks=1&newbks_redir=0&sa=X&ved=2ahUKEwjH9se60J6FAxXy8zgGHWcAC9IQ6AF6BAgKEAI#v=onepage&q=salitang%20%22Hokkien%22&f=false
|text={{nb...}} Beinte-singko anyos lang si Kho Bunthong. Siya ang pinakabata sa editorial department. Nakapag-aral siya ng journalism sa isang unibersidad sa Tsina, at mahusay pang mag-Ingles, kaya siya ang inatasang maging reporter ni Mr. Le. Makuwento siya at prangka magsalita, maliksi kumilos at mabilis magtrabaho, at tapat sa pakikiharap sa tao, kaya't natutuwa sa kanya ang mga taga-editorial department. Galing siya sa Chaozhou sa Guangdong. Maliban sa pagkakaiba sa punto ay hindi gaanong nagkakalayo ang salitang Chaozhou sa '''Hokkien'''. Siguro'y dahil hindi masyadong malayo ang aming edad, gayundin ang pag-uugali, kaya naman madali kaming {{nb...}}|t={{nb...}} Kho Bunthong is only twenty-five years old. He is the youngest in the editorial department. He studied journalism at a university in China, and speaks English well, so he was assigned to be a reporter for Mr. Le. He is talkative and straightforward, agile and quick to work, and honest in dealing with people, which is why the editorial department likes him. He is from Chaozhou in Guangdong. Except to a certain point, the Chaozhou language is not that far from '''Hokkien'''. Maybe it's because our age is not too far apart, as well as our behavior, that's why we easily {{nb...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|tl|year=2012|title=Philippine Social Sciences Review|volume=64|issue=2|publisher=College of Social Sciences and Philosophy, University of the Philippines|page=25|url=https://books.google.com.ph/books?newbks=1&newbks_redir=0&id=UMt6sMSbEyoC&dq=taong+%22Hokkien%22&focus=searchwithinvolume&q=%22Hokkien%22
|text={{nb...}} nakapagsasalita ng '''Hokkien''' o Cantonese. Sa struktura ng pamilya, mas sumusunod din ang mga mestiso sa kagawiang Pilipino na nagbibigay-halaga sa kamag-anak ng babaeng asawa. Kapansin-pansin naman sa kanila ang ilang katangian tulad ng pagkahilig sa karangyaan, debosyon sa Katolisismo at sa kulturang Espanyol, at kakayahang magnegosyo {{nb...}}|t={{nb...}} able to speak '''Hokkien''' or Cantonese. In the family structure, the mestizos also follow the Filipino practice that gives importance to the wife's relatives. They are notable for some characteristics such as a penchant for luxury, devotion to Catholicism and Spanish culture, and the ability to do business {{nb...}}}}

===Adjective===
{{tl-adj|b=+}}

# Of or relating to the {{l|en|Hokkien}} language
#: {{syn|tl|Fukien|Amoy}}

{{C|tl|Languages}}
