===Etymology 1===
{{bor+|tl|es|i}}. Each pronunciation has a different source:
* Filipino alphabet pronunciation is influenced by {{der|tl|en|i}}.
* Abakada alphabet pronunciation is influenced by [[Baybayin]] character {{m|tl|ᜁ}}.
* Abecedario pronunciation is from {{der|tl|es|i}}.

====Pronunciation====
{{tl-pr
|áy<qq:letter name, Filipino alphabet>
|í<qq:letter name, Abakada alphabet, Abecedario>
|-í<qq:phoneme, stressed>
|-i<qq:phoneme, unstressed>
|syll=i
}}

====Letter====
{{tl-letter|b=ay}}

# {{tl-letter-def|9|ay|alph=fil}}

====Letter====
{{tl-letter|b=i}}

# {{tl-letter-def|8|i|alph=abak}}
# {{lb|tl|historical}} {{tl-letter-def|10|i|alph=abec}}

=====See also=====
* {{list:Latin script letters/tl/simple}}

====Noun====
{{tl-noun|b=+}}

# {{tl-letter name|I|i|alph=abak}}
#: {{syn|tl|ay|q1=in the Filipino alphabet}}
# {{lb|tl|historical}} {{tl-letter name|I|i|alph=abec}}
#: {{syn|tl|ay|q1=in the Filipino alphabet}}

=====See also=====
* {{l|tl|ii}}

===Etymology 2===
{{bor+|tl|en|ee}}, the English name of the letter {{m|en|E}}/{{m|en|e}}.

====Pronunciation====
{{tl-pr}}

====Noun====
{{tl-noun|b=+}}

# {{tl-letter name|E|e|alph=fil}}
#: {{syn|tl|e|q1=in the Abakada alphabet and Abecedario}}

=====See also=====
* {{list:Latin script letter names/tl/simple}}

===Further reading===
* {{R:Pambansang Diksiyonaryo}}

