Page 7309 Intsik beho, tulo laway: Would save with comment = convert {{tl-IPA}} to {{tl-pr}}; remove/incorporate {{hyph}} into {{tl-pr}}
Page 7309 Intsik beho, tulo laway: -------- begin text --------

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|tl|Intsik viejo, tulo laway}}

===Etymology===
{{lit|old and decrepit Chinese, drooling saliva}}, said without verbal conjugation to imitate a stereotypical Chinese accent in Tagalog. Seen as a childhood rhyme, this expression probably originated in the late 1800s during the {{w|History of the Philippines (1565–1898)|Spanish colonial period}} of the Philippines, when opium dens were common and many Chinese immigrants would be left decrepit and drooling saliva from opium. Compare {{cog|ceb|Insik wakang, kaon, kalibang}}.

===Pronunciation===
{{tl-pr|Intsík beho, tulò laway}}

===Phrase===
{{tl-head|phrase|[[Intsík]] [[beho]], [[tulò]] [[laway]]|b=Intsik beho / tulo laway}}

# {{lb|tl|slang|offensive|archaic}} {{n-g|taunt used to insult a Chinese person}}

====Derived terms====
* {{l|tl|Intsik beho}}

====Related terms====
* {{l|tl|Intsik batutay, tulo-tulo laway}}
