Page 9239 konyo: -------- begin text --------

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|tl|conyo}}
* {{alt|tl|coño}}

===Etymology===
Possibly from {{bor|tl|es|coño||cunt; pussy}} or {{noncog|es|de nuevo cuño|t=(of a person) who has recently entered a profession, union or social class.}}. The sense referring to “coming from a wealthy family” is said to have come from high-class people who curse “Coño!” during the 1950s. Compare {{cog|en|conyo}}. See also {{noncog|en|valley girl}}, {{noncog|es|fresa}}.

===Pronunciation===
{{tl-pr}}

===Noun===
{{tl-noun|b=+}} {{tlb|tl|slang}}

# {{lb|tl|slightly|_|derogatory}} a person who speaks in a [[pretentious]] and [[bratty]] way such as having an [[unnatural]] manner of [[code-mixing]] between [[Tagalog]] and [[English]] or speaking in an [[accent]] associated to a [[privilege]]d [[social class]]
# a person who belongs to wealthy English-speaking families
# a wealthy [[Anglophone]] person, often raised or living in a [[subdivision]] [[village]] ([[gated community]])
# {{lb|tl|archaic}} a person who belongs to wealthy Spanish-speaking families

====Related terms====
{{rel3|tl|konyito|konyita|Konyo}}

===Interjection===
{{tl-head|interjection|b=+}} {{tlb|tl|archaic|vulgar}}

# {{n-g|Expression of worry, failure, shock, displeasure, surprise, etc.}}: [[damn]]!

====See also====
* {{l|tl|puta}}
