Page 10666 liyempo: -------- begin text --------

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|tl|liempo}}
* {{alt|tl|yempo}}
* {{alt|tl|liampo|liyampo||obsolete}}

===Etymology===
{{bor+|tl|nan-hbl|-}}, possibly from:
* {{m|nan-hbl|奶脯|tr=leng-pó͘ |pos={{IPAchar|/liɪŋ⁴⁴⁻²² pɔ²³/}}|lit=dried breast meat, usually, of an animal}},<ref name="tdj"/><ref name="chhoetaigi"/> which Manuel (1948) defined as {{compound|nan-hbl|奶|tr1=leng|t1=milk; udder; breast|脯|tr2=pô͘|t2=udder of any animal|nocat=1}}.<ref name="Manuel"/> Lim (1941) records the same word romanized as “''lin po''” with the characters, {{zh-l|[[乳]][[部]]|lit=breast region}}.<ref name="Lim"/> Compare {{noncog|nan-hbl|奶|tr=lin|t=breast}}, {{zh-l|脯|tr=pó͘|t=[[dried]] [[meat]]}}, {{zh-l|肉脯|tr=bah-pó͘|t=[[dried]] (and [[processed]]) [[meat]]}}, {{zh-l|撚部尾|tr=lián-pō͘-bé|t=lower abdomen}}.<ref name="tdj2"/>
* {{compound|nan-hbl|鐮刀|tr1=liâm-to|t1=sickle|肉|tr2=bah|t2=meat|nocat=1}}, which Chan-Yap (1980) defined the former as “stomach portion of pig”,<ref name="Chan-Yap"/> likely referring to the [[streaky]] appearance of [[pork belly]]. Compare {{noncog|cmn|鐮刀|tr=liándāo}}, {{noncog|nan-hbl|腳廉刀|tr=kha-liâm-to|t=[[tibia]]; [[shinbone]]}}.
Zorc (1985) records both etymologies but finds Manuel (1948)'s as less likely.<ref name="CEDOF"/>

===Pronunciation===
{{tl-pr}}

===Noun===
{{tl-noun|b=+}}

# [[pork belly]]; [[streaky]] [[pork]]

