===Etymology===
From {{rdp|tl|nocap=1}} of {{der|tl|en|mix}}. Popularized by Indian Filipino vlogger {{m-g|Mr. Nobodydudy}} (Navjot Singh) by being a viral social media trend on October 2023 to describe how street food were prepared by hand.<ref>{{cite-journal|date=2023 October 11|title=Meaning behind the viral word ‘Mekus Mekus’|journal=Manila Bulletin|url=https://mb.com.ph/2023/10/11/read-meaning-behind-the-viral-word-mekus-mekus}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
{{tl-pr|+|mekës-mekës<qq:Indian pronunciation influence>}}

===Noun===
{{tl-noun|b=+}} {{tlb|tl|slang}}

# act of [[mixing]]
#: {{syn|tl|halo}}
#* {{quote-journal|tl|date=2023-10-11|quotee=Mr. Nobodydudy|title=Meaning behind the viral word ‘Mekus Mekus’|journal=Manila Bulletin|location=New Delhi|passage='''Mekus Mekus''', Mix Mix ha. Kami na Indian we cannot pronounce it straight. Baka 'yung iba kaya nila 'yan, ako hindi.|t='''''Mekus Mekus''''', is Mix Mix, ok? We Indians cannot pronounce it correctly. Maybe others can, but I can’t.|brackets=on}}
# {{lb|tl|figurative}} [[extra]] [[effort]], [[speed]], or [[skill]] when [[perform]]ing a [[task]]
#: {{syn|tl|lakas|galing|husay}}

====Derived terms====
{{col-auto|tl|imekus-mekus|magmekus-mekus}}

