Page 14056 oo: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|tl|poz-pro|*həqə|t=yes; expression of agreement}}.<ref>{{R:map:ACD|heqe}}</ref> Compare {{cog|bya|ee}}, {{cog|ceb|oo}}, {{cog|hil|hoo}}, and {{cog|tsg|huun}}.

====Pronunciation====
{{tl-pr|o7o|u7o<qq:dialectal>|ë7ë<qq:relaxed>}}

====Interjection====
{{tl-head|interjection|b=+}}

# {{lb|tl|informal}} [[yes]]
#: {{syn|tl|ao|q1=Bataan}}
#: {{ant|tl|hindi}}

=====Usage notes=====
* The term {{m|tl|opo}} and {{m|tl|oho}} is used instead as an honorific when talking to elders, superiors, or even strangers to show politeness.
* The relaxed pronunciation can also be used to denote that the listener is still listening or is still interested in what the speaker is saying. ({{w|Backchannel (linguistics)|backchanneling}})

=====Alternative forms=====
* {{alt|tl|huo||Rizal}}

=====Derived terms=====
{{der3|tl|umoo|magpaoo|paoohan|oohan|mapaoo|paoohin|opo|oho|pag-oo|pagpapaoo|tagaoo}}

===Etymology 2===

====Pronunciation====
{{tl-pr|o7ò}}

====Noun====
{{tl-noun|oò|b=+}}

# {{lb|tl|colloquial}} [[feces]]; [[excrement]]; [[dung]]
#: {{syn|tl|tae|dumi|q2=formal, euphemistic|jebs|q3=slang|ebak|q4=slang|pupu|q5=childish|ipot|q6=of fowl}}

=====Derived terms=====
* {{l|tl|umoo}}

===Etymology 3===

====Pronunciation====
{{tl-pr|o7ô}}

====Noun====
{{tl-noun|oô|b=+}}

# {{obsolete form of|tl|uo}}

