Page 14076 opo: Removing explicit syllabification same as auto-syllabification: {{hyph|tl|o|po}}
Page 14076 opo: Replaced {{tl-IPA|opò}} with {{tl-pr|opò}}
Page 14076 opo: Would save with comment = convert {{tl-IPA}} to {{tl-pr}}; remove {{hyph}} same as respelling auto-syllabification
Page 14076 opo: -------- begin text --------
{{phrasebook|tl}}

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|tl|upo||rare}}

===Etymology===
Possibly from the {{univerbation|tl|oo|po|nocap=1}}.

===Pronunciation===
{{tl-pr|opò}}

===Interjection===
{{tl-head|interjection|opò|b=+}}

# {{lb|tl|honorific}} [[yes]]
#: {{syn|tl|oho}}
#: {{ant|tl|hindi po}}

====Usage notes====
* In standard Tagalog, ''opo'' is used as an honorific towards elders, superiors, and even strangers, while ''oho'' is a slightly less formal honorific that can be used for intimate elders and superiors. However, the difference between the two has blurred in recent decades. In other dialects, there is no difference between the two at all and can be interchanged with one another.

====Derived terms====
{{der3|tl|mag-opo|mamupo|umopo}}

====See also====
* {{l|tl|oo}}

===Further reading===
* {{R:Pambansang Diksiyonaryo}}

