[[File:Perrachica.jpg|thumb|[[worn out]] 5¢ {{m|es|perra chica|lit=little [[bitch]] (dog)}} coin, 1870]]

===Etymology===
Either from {{bor|tl|es|perra|| 5 and 10 [[centimo]] [[peseta]] [[coins]]|lit=[[bitch]] / [[female]] [[dog]]}},<ref name="Potet"/> or {{bor|tl|es|pela||1 [[peseta]] [[coins]]|lit=a [[hit]] or [[beating]]}}, due to the circulation of {{m|es|perra gorda||10 [[centimo]] [[peseta]] coin}}, {{m|es|perra chica||5 [[centimo]] [[peseta]] coin}}, and {{m|es|pela||1 peseta coin}}, coins which depicted a [[Spanish]] [[heraldic]] ''[[lion]] [[rampant]] [[regardant]]'' during the {{w|History of the Philippines (1565–1898)|Spanish colonial period}} in the [[Philippines]].

Potet (2016) also remarks that it seems to be a cross between {{cog|ms|perak}} and {{cog|es|perra}}, wherein an [[interference]] could've occurred between the colloquial senses of {{noncog|es|perra|t=[[cash]]; [[dough]] (money)|lit=[[bitch]] (dog)}} and {{noncog|ms|perak|t=[[coin]]|lit=[[silver]]}}. See also {{m|tl|pilak}}.

===Pronunciation===
{{tl-pr|+|pira<qq:Bataan>}}

===Noun===
{{tl-noun|b=+}}

# [[money]]; [[cash]]
#: {{syn|tl|Thesaurus:salapi}}

====Derived terms====
{{der3|tl|perahan|mapera|mamera|perahin|sampera|mukhang pera}}

===References===
<references>
<ref name="Potet">{{R:tl:Potet|TBC|page=136}}</ref>
</references>

-------- end text --------
