[[File:White_h_2.svg |thumb|''The Two of Hearts is the highest-ranking card in Pusoy Dos'']]

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|tl|posoy dos}}
* {{alt|tl|posoy-dos}}

===Etymology===
From {{compound|tl|pusoy|dos}}, where the former is from {{compound|tl|puso|t1=heart (card)|y|lang2=es|t2=and}}, while the latter is from {{bor|tl|es|dos|t=two (card)}}. Compare {{m|tl|treynta y dos}}, {{m|tl|setenta y dos}}, {{m|tl|sesenta y dos}}<br>The card game itself is of Chinese origin despite the name. Compare {{cog|id|capsa susun}}, {{cog|nan-hbl|十三|tr=cha̍p-saⁿ}}, {{cog|vi|tiến lên}}, {{cog|yue|[[鋤大弟]] / [[鋤大D]]|tr=co<sup>4</sup> daai<sup>6</sup> di<sup>2</sup>}}.

===Pronunciation===
{{tl-pr|pusóy dos}}

===Noun===
{{tl-noun|[[pusóy]] [[dos]]|b=+}}

# {{lb|tl|card games}} [[big two]]; [[big deuce]]; [[Dai Di]] {{gloss|a shedding type card game of Chinese origin, especially the Philippine variant}}
#: {{syn|tl|chikicha|sikitcha}}
# {{lb|tl|card games}} [[poker]] {{gloss|especially the Philippine variant}}
#: {{syn|tl|chikicha|sikitcha}}

====Derived terms====
{{der3|tl|pusoy}}

====See also====
* {{l|tl|tong its}}
* {{l|tl|kuwaho}}

===Further reading===
