Page 16301 puto-seko: Removing explicit syllabification same as auto-syllabification: {{hyph|tl|pu|to-se|ko}}
Page 16301 puto-seko: Replaced {{tl-IPA}} with {{tl-pr}}
Page 16301 puto-seko: Would save with comment = convert {{tl-IPA}} to {{tl-pr}}; remove {{hyph}} same as respelling auto-syllabification
Page 16301 puto-seko: -------- begin text --------

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|tl|puto seco}}
* {{alt|tl|puto seko}}
* {{alt|tl|putoseko}}
* {{alt|tl|putuseko}}

===Etymology===
From {{w|Philippine Spanish|Philippine}} {{bor|tl|es|poto seco}}, where {{der|tl|es|seco|t=dry}} and {{w|Philippine Spanish|Philippine}} {{cog|es|poto}} itself is from {{cog|tl|puto}} which comes from {{der|tl|ms|putu}}, which is from {{der|tl|ta|புட்டு||a South Indian Tamil pastry}}. {{surf|tl|puto|seko|t2=dry}}.

===Pronunciation===
{{tl-pr}}

===Noun===
{{tl-noun|[[puto]]-[[seko]]|b=+}}

# {{w|puto seco}} {{gl|a [[sweet]] [[rice cake]] [[pastry]] [[delicacy]] made up of [[sugar]], [[cornstarch]], [[rice flour]], [[egg]], and [[butter]], but is [[drier]] unlike {{m|tl|puto}}, which is cooked steamed and is softer in texture}}
#: {{syn|tl|puto masa}}

====Usage notes====
Usage in Tagalog/Filipino or in the Philippines in general is neither offensive nor vulgar, unlike when read in Spanish which it should not be mistaken with {{cog|es|puto}}. The term is a popular [[pasalubong]] (a souvenir item to bring home) among Filipinos.

===Further reading===
* {{R:KWF Diksiyonaryo}}
* http://www.shellyviajeratravel.com/2017/08/filipino-phrases-borrowed-from-spanish.html
