[[Image:Small pitcher Iran Louvre MAO714.jpg|thumb|right|Isang '''saro''' para pangbuhos ng likido (A '''pitcher jug''' for pouring liquids).]]

===Etymology===
{{glossary|Borrowed}} from (c. 16th-18th century) {{w|Early Modern Spanish|Early Modern}} {{bor|tl|es|jarro}}. In {{w|Early Modern Spanish}}, Spanish {{angbr|j}} was pronounced {{IPAchar|/ʃ/}}; {{IPAchar|/ʃ/}} became {{IPAchar|/s/}} as common with other early borrowings (compare {{m|tl|sabon}}, {{m|tl|singkamas}}, {{m|tl|sugarol}}, {{m|tl|sugal}}, and {{m|tl|tasa}}). {{doublet|tl|haro}}.

===Pronunciation===
{{tl-pr}}

===Noun===
{{tl-noun|b=+}}

# [[jar]] or [[jug]] made of [[clay]] or [[porcelain]] {{q|especially for water}}
#: {{syn|tl|pitsel|haro|}}
# [[earthenware]] [[mug]]
#: {{syn|tl|taro}}

====See also====
{{col3|tl|kalalang|galong|gulgurita}}

{{C|tl|Vessels}}

