From (c. 16th-18th century) {{w|Early Modern Spanish|Early Modern}} {{bor|tl|es|tajar||to cut}}, from {{der|tl|LL.|taliare}}, present active infinitive of {{m|la|talio||I cut}}. In {{w|Early Modern Spanish}}, Spanish {{angbr|j}} was pronounced {{IPAchar|/ʃ/}}; {{IPAchar|/ʃ/}} became {{IPAchar|/s/}} as common with other early borrowings (compare {{m|tl|sabon}}, {{m|tl|singkamas}}, {{m|tl|sugal}}, {{m|tl|sugarol}}, and {{m|tl|saro}}).

====Pronunciation====
{{tl-pr|tasá}}

====Noun====
{{tl-noun|tasá|b=+}}

# [[act]] of [[sharpen]]ing {{q|of something blunt, such as a blade, pencil, etc.}}
# [[sharpened]] [[point]] {{q|of something previously blunt, such as a blade, pencil, etc.}}

=====Derived terms=====
{{der3|tl|pagtatasa|magtasa|pantasa|tasahan}}

====Adjective====
{{tl-adj|tasá|b=+}}

# [[sharpened]] {{q|of a pointed object that has become blunt}}

===Etymology 3===
{{bor+|tl|es|tasa}}.

====Pronunciation====
{{tl-pr}}

====Noun====
