Page 20633 uway: Would save with comment = convert {{tl-IPA}} to {{tl-pr}}; remove {{hyph}} same as respelling auto-syllabification
Page 20633 uway: -------- begin text --------

===Etymology===
From {{inh|tl|map-pro|*quay}} (cf. {{cog|ami|'oway}}, {{cog|dtp|tuai}}, {{cog|ebk|owoy}}, {{cog|iba|wi}}, {{cog|ckv|uway}}, {{cog|pwn|quai}}, {{cog|rad|hwiê}}, {{cog|szy|uway}}, {{cog|tsg|uay}}, and {{cog|war|uway}}).

===Pronunciation===
{{tl-pr|uwáy}}

===Noun===
{{tl-noun|uwáy|b=+}}

# [[rattan]] {{gl|palm, stem, and canes made}}
#: {{syn|tl|yantok|palasan|behuko}}

====Usage notes====
* According to English (1987),<ref>{{R:tl:Leo James English|page=1555}}</ref> '''uway''' is used for chairs and beds, {{m|tl|yantok}} for binding nipa roofing or tying bamboo scaffolding, and {{m|tl|palasan}} is a thick type of rattan. However, these distinctions may not be applicable everywhere, as in some places, {{m|tl|yantok}} is the plant, {{m|tl|uway}} are rattan strips, and {{m|tl|palasan}} are rattan sticks not made to strips but usually intended to make cane and cane furniture.

====Derived terms====
* {{l|tl|baling-uway}}

====See also====
{{col3|tl|ubakan|tumalula|ubakan}}

===References===
<references/>

-------- end text --------
